Kamalie native host (@kamalie-native-host: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @ws-kamalie-games) {
Retained architecture exploration. The selected game direction and initial scope are in Standalone-castle.lingua. Older references to in-game UI and undecided viewpoint are superseded by that owner decision.

## First delivery — isolated native Castle

The owner's priority is an optimized game with room to discover what makes it enjoyable. Begin with the actual Castle in the actual canvas and a tiny interactive Bevy scene inside it. Iterate on movement, combat, builds, feedback and encounters in that contained form. Direct Bevy systems, components, UI, assets, animation, shaders and game-specific physics/controllers are available within the chosen runtime configuration.

The outer Castle is an ordinary Lince element. The inner game owns its map, actors, camera, clock, collision participation, random state and run lifecycle. Its first gameplay implementation uses its own direct Bevy mechanics. Areas, attraction, Karma, Possession and native behavior recipes become later integration providers. The game library's dependency boundary keeps Record and Castle types in the Lince adapter.

### The first native workflow

1. Add Kamalie through the normal native add/store path and place it in an ordinary workspace.
2. Move, resize, select, layer and pin the outer Castle through the element controls that the host supports. Workspace navigation and editing continue through their normal input context.
3. Focus the game viewport and play. Keyboard, pointer and supported gamepad input reach the selected session. The game camera and actors move within their local map.
4. Use a visible release action or its configured keyboard equivalent to return to normal workspace interaction. Clicking another component, opening a workspace tool, changing workspace or losing application focus releases held game input and pointer capture.
5. Pause, resume, restart and stop through discoverable controls. Display the effective state and choose the unfocused/hidden policy during the first host experiment. Show the selected unattended/background behavior and measure its resource demand.
6. Save and reopen the Castle's placement and settings. Offer an explicit supported run checkpoint/resume path. Removal releases the instance's active CPU/GPU resources, audio, input capture and jobs according to the existing save/delete semantics.
7. Add a second Kamalie Castle and verify independent input, run state, random streams, clocks, cameras and teardown.

### Ownership, isolation and optimization

Resolve the actual execution boundary during the first small host experiment. Scoped entities in the host App can share GPU assets efficiently when game state, clocks and collision are correctly isolated. A separate World can own conflicting game resources and solver state, with an explicit rendering bridge. An independent executable remains an available engineering option when its engine/resource needs justify process integration. The initial product acceptance always exercises a normal native Castle in Lince.

Prove isolation for inputs, queries, contacts, state writes and cleanup. Visibility layers are one part of the rendering arrangement. Game physics must have a supported owned schedule and configuration. When its solver needs resources that conflict with the host's Avian setup, choose an isolated runtime or another verified configuration during this first experiment. Pausing or changing game gravity affects the game's owned simulation. The outer Castle continues through ordinary workspace placement and host policies.

Keep hot loops in compact Bevy data, retain GPU-side rendering, reuse assets where ownership permits it, and measure host/render overhead early. The game can use native Bevy UI and directly authored game data to change loadouts, tune mechanics and compare builds. The first implementation can evolve these definitions rapidly while recording save-format versions and explicit migration/restart behavior. Later shared authoring should consume the useful operators discovered here.

### Readiness and parallel work

The first deliverable consumes the existing native element creation, placement, rendering, focus/input and lifecycle paths, together with the game package. Fix concrete host gaps within that bounded scope as they appear. The 26-package map below describes available future ingredients. Their named provider joins apply when those integrations are undertaken.

Complete KAM.T00, KAM.T01, KAM.T02 and the first-delivery scope of KAM.T07. Prove KAM.W00, KAM.W01, the contained portions of KAM.W03/KAM.W04, KAM.P00 and the applicable KAM.P02 regressions. This establishes a usable isolated Castle milestone. Area workflows, shared recipe editing and cross-mode comparisons have their own later completion boundary.

Begin with one owner for the small host/input/runtime boundary. Once a real focused scene works and that boundary is stable, gameplay/content experiments and host lifecycle/performance work can proceed in separate leaf modules. A standalone runner is an optional harness within this work. Give shared renderer, input and solver edits one owner at a time.

## Source evidence and current limits

This is source inspection of the working tree on the date above. The checkout contains ongoing runtime changes. Implementation must record and test the actual revision it consumes. Native execution and performance comparisons remain pending.

- The interface pins Bevy 0.19.1 and Avian3D 0.7.0 with double-precision physics. A Castle is already a Bevy component requiring a UI Node. Lince builds a Bevy App and registers its native features as plugins. This permits a small Castle host around ordinary Bevy gameplay. [Dependencies](../../../../../../crates/interface/Cargo.toml) · [Castle](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/castle.rs) · [Application](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/app.rs)
- Park creates external application surfaces as Castle, CanvasItem, WorkspaceMember and ApplicationWindow entities backed by GPU image materials. Firefox already has a presentation and placement that an adapter can reference. Turning that presentation into a game participant still needs collision, lifecycle and authority integration. [Application surfaces](../../../../../../crates/park/src/presentation/clients.rs)
- Workspace physics already uses Avian, force Areas, spatial bodies and terrain. Spatial camera navigation contains move-and-slide collision. Character grounding, jumping, combat and game-specific movement rules still require implementation. [Physics](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/physics.rs) · [Spatial physics](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/topology/physics.rs) · [Navigation](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/topology/view.rs)
- Feature-gated Freedoom already provides an embedded game's screen, controls, focus and lifecycle. Its process and CPU frame-upload path supplies a hosting precedent. The proposed native Bevy host needs its own GPU rendering proof. [Freedoom](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/freedoom.rs)
- Karma has bounded expression evaluation and execution policies. Its current tracing and validation costs need measurement before using it at gameplay frequency. Existing Areas provide attraction, property filters and immunity. [Karma evaluation](../../../../../../crates/nucleus/src/karma/evaluate.rs) · [Area effects](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/area_effects.rs)
- Kamalie's combat, procedural dungeon generation, enemy AI, build system, run saves and game-session integration remain new work. Pending Workspace packages specify additional reusable contracts and verification beyond today's implementations.

## Three independent architecture choices

| Choice | Supported alternatives to explore | Consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Presentation | Contained viewport, workspace camera, view-relative HUD | Determines where the scene is displayed and how input is mapped |
| Environment | Generated game map, enrolled workspace objects, a declared combination | Determines what can collide, supply effects and receive game actions |
| Execution | Scoped entities in the host App, a separate ECS World, an independent executable | Determines resource ownership, engine compatibility and integration cost |

A contained viewport can display a fully native Bevy simulation. A workspace-area game can keep its enemies and projectiles as compact ECS entities. A camera can show an existing session through several views when its presentation contract supports that. Changing a view preserves the underlying session. Moving a run between different environments requires an explicit validated transition for positions, participants, state and active effects.

## Proposed code boundaries

The following module names illustrate responsibility. Select the smallest interfaces while implementing the isolated Castle. Refine environment-provider boundaries when the later area adapter has concrete requirements. The diagram shows the intended eventual composition.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    G[Kamalie Bevy gameplay]
    S[Optional standalone runner] --> G
    C[First delivery - isolated Castle adapter] --> G
    A[Later workspace area adapter] --> G
    C --> V[Native views, input and lifecycle]
    A --> V
    A --> P[Participants, queries and physics ownership]
    A --> O[Supported Lince actions and authored content]
```

### Gameplay package

`kamalie` owns the actual Bevy systems for actors, combat, targeting, projectiles, ability effects, loot, enemies, navigation, encounters and run state. Its library imports Bevy and chosen game libraries. Inspect and test its dependency boundary independently of Lince's Record and Castle crates. Exercise actual gameplay first through the isolated Castle. A standalone runner can additionally demonstrate independent application execution.

Use one implementation of game rules with explicit game input, environment data, effects and save state. Begin with the local map, direct ECS systems and the exchanges required by the isolated host. The later integrated environment can motivate providers for obstacle queries, field samples, interaction handles and supported effect requests. Introduce traits where an implemented substitution needs them. Ordinary ECS queries and typed components remain available to the game as its design develops.

An optional standalone runner owns its App construction and engine plugins. The initial Lince adapter connects the game to the selected compatible runtime and supplies actual presentation, focus and lifecycle exports. Game systems can use custom shaders, meshes, animation, ECS queries and supported Bevy extensions. Shared App resources, rendering configuration and solver schedules have explicit owners.

### Native host and instances

`kamalie_lince` owns the Castle shell, workspace placement, session start/stop, input handoff, camera target, admission and saved game reference. Register the game plugin once per App and create sessions as runtime instances. Keep each instance's state, random stream, entities, inputs, cameras, collision participation and teardown scoped. A global resource containing one player or one run would break two simultaneous Castles.

Only selected objects need exported workspace identity, inspection, placement or connections. Hundreds of enemies, damage numbers and short-lived projectiles can remain game-owned entities. Save a definition/revision, run checkpoint and selected outcomes through the existing persistence service at meaningful boundaries. Reuse the durable model's R0543 principle of preparing typed data outside hot loops. [Execution contracts](../contracts/Durable-model-contracts.lingua)

### Execution alternatives and full Bevy control

In-process hosting shares the renderer and assets and can avoid cross-process transport. The game must cooperate with Lince's Bevy version, patched renderer, input routing, time resources and physics ownership. A compiled Bevy plugin is a code integration artifact. The current Custom Castle composition format would need an explicit runtime integration to launch such a plugin.

A separate ECS World or SubApp gives independent component/resource state. Rendering it through the host still needs extraction or a deliberate renderer integration. Asset handles, cameras and physics contacts need actual cross-world mappings. Visual RenderLayers select camera visibility. Simulation isolation requires entity/query ownership, clocks and physical-domain rules in addition to visibility. Bevy documents the SubApp's separate World and its explicit extraction step. [Bevy SubApp 0.19.1](https://docs.rs/bevy/0.19.1/bevy/app/struct.SubApp.html)

An independent Bevy executable controls its own engine version, plugin set, renderer and process lifetime. Park can present its normal application window using the existing app path. Area interaction then needs IPC, participant snapshots and effect exchange. This option is useful when independent engine configuration is required. Compare its actual rendering and transport costs before selecting it for performance.

## Performance, control and fidelity

The Castle marker and a thin host boundary have measurable costs, but neither imposes a requirement to represent every runtime entity as editable UI. Main costs depend on simulation work, rendering, synchronization, allocation and publication choices. The integrated form adds participant queries, proxy maintenance and live surfaces. A contained host can add a render target and composition pass. An independent process can add transport and duplicated resource residency. Their relative costs require comparison on the same workload.

Reuse compact Bevy components, prepared definition data, spatial indices and change-driven participant updates. Batch supported effects across the host boundary. Keep per-tick combat and movement out of Record serialization, full-workspace scans and heavy proof/tracing paths. Existing surface/GPU resources should be reused when ownership and device compatibility permit it. Avoid CPU pixel round trips in the native render path.

Full access to Bevy APIs is compatible with a library that runs inside Lince. Shared execution still entails coordination over app-global resources and scheduling. Game developers retain direct systems, queries and rendering extensions within those agreements. A request for a conflicting global renderer, engine version or physics clock can select a stronger execution boundary.

Maintain the Workspace fidelity axes independently. Visual quality can change materials, effects and mesh detail while keeping combat geometry and rules stable. A complex object can use a cheap collision shape and a functional ability interface. Background enemies can use an implemented cheaper behavior model with declared observable differences. Adaptive physics steps, simplified contacts or AI updates can change gameplay outcomes, so transitions need an explicit policy and evidence. Offscreen objects retain simulation demand when they can still affect the run. Camera visibility alone cannot decide activity lifetime.

The current physics integration is a concrete constraint. It installs Avian on a private WorkspaceStep schedule, uses global configuration and gravity, advances approximately 120 times per second with bounded catch-up, and filters contacts using known workspace body types. Integration needs a real participant extension at that boundary. Registering a second default solver in the same App or adding an arbitrary collider does not establish correct shared contacts. Prefer one authoritative solver for mutually interacting bodies. An isolated simulation can import static/kinematic obstacle proxies, while two-way dynamic coupling needs an explicit supported force/state exchange. [Current physics integration](../../../../../../crates/interface/src/native/physics.rs)
}
